Report sections this topic could honestly feed. No copy is written here.

1. One bipolar rail in Gregory's own words: 'quiet mind' and 'restless mind' (Moralia VI.57). Name both poles and give no percentage. The method page should say plainly that the Fathers rank contemplation higher in merit and put action first in time. The rail shows which road is safer for your make-up, not who is better.

2. 'What you might not have noticed', drawn from each side's hidden danger:
- quiet: faults asleep, not gone; untested virtue; a buried gift (Cassian 19.12; Chrysostom VI.6-8; Basil, Longer Rules 7; Pastoral Rule I.5);
- restless: quiet makes your thoughts worse; the inner life starves; overreach (Moralia VI.57; Pastoral Rule II.7).

3. 'How it disguises itself':
- seclusion taken for discipline (Chrysostom VI.7);
- business 'sought after out of affection for the things themselves' in the name of duty (Pastoral Rule II.7);
- refusal passing as humility (Pastoral Rule I.6).

4. 'What helps you':
- love moves the quiet to work, and fear anchors the restless in prayer (Moralia VI.58);
- test yourself in action first (VI.59);
- go in and out like Moses (Pastoral Rule II.5);
- Augustine's two rules (City of God XIX.19).

5. A second reading, 'when you are called': Isaiah or Jeremiah (Pastoral Rule I.7; Oration 2.114). Gregory praises both.

6. 'Your line in the Body':
- Cassian's list of lines (Conference 14.4) and Nastir's Abraham, David and Elijah (Budge no. 605);
- Cassian's warning against copying others (14.5-7);
- Basil's point that no one has every gift.
This is the natural cross-link to the spiritual gifts quiz. It answers the owner's 'who you are in the Body of Christ'.

7. 'Kindred figures' with real citations, in the spirit of 'famous people who share your type':
- Mary and Martha; Rachel and Leah; Jeremiah and Isaiah (Gregory);
- Elijah and Abraham (Nastir);
- Arsenius and Moses the Ethiopian (the two boats);
- Gregory Nazianzen, who calls himself a lover of 'calm and retirement' (Oration 2.6).
Avoid claims about Gregory the Great's own temperament unless his letters are checked first.

8. A 'worth watching' cross-link to the existing Book III reading. Gregory's 'listless/torpid' versus 'unquiet' pair (Pastoral Rule III.36) looks like the same contrast. Unless the Latin of III.36 is checked, the main session must present that link as its own reading.
